Improving Your Skills
=====================

Improving your weapon skills and spell casting skills is an area that
takes a little care. You can earn up to 29 "skill slots", one per
increase in experience level. It turns out that all of the character
classes have far more possible skill improvements in weapons and spell
casting than available skill slots. So you should prepare a plan and
#enhance your skills as you gain experience per that plan.

You can advance a skill only if you have exercised the skill enough and
have available skill slots. The amount of exercise and number of slots
also increases as the skill level increases. For all but riding, bare
handed combat, and martial arts:
 o unskilled to basic - 20 successful spells or attacks (> 1HP damage);
one skill slot
 o basic to skilled - 60 more (80 total) successful spells or attacks;
two skill slots for weapons, one for spells
 o skilled to expert - 100 more (180 total) successful spells or
attacks; three skill slots for weapons, two for spells
The other three skills have slightly reduced slot usage plus higher
levels (master, grand master) available. See the weapon skills table in
  http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/343/weap-343.html
for more details along with the benefits (+ to hit / + damage) conveyed
for all weapon skills.

Note also that when you #twoweapon, you only train that skill (not the
skills of the weapons being used). As a result, improve your the
individual weapon skills first, then start to #twoweapon to improve
that skill.

The effects of several spells (cone of cold, confuse monster, detect
food, detect monsters, detect treasure, fireball, haste self, jumping,
levitation, protection, remove curse, restore ability) will change
between the basic and skilled levels, see
  http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/343/spl2-343.html
for details. Note that improving your spell skill to Expert will
further reduce failure rates but not change the spell further.

The following are included to give you ideas for the three recommended
starting classes. The organization of the information is:
 - starting skills for weapons and spells
 - weapon skills you can advance to master / expert / skilled
 - spell skills you can advance to expert / skilled
 - early game suggestions
 - mid to late game suggestions

*Valkyrie* Starts with basic in dagger and long sword and unskilled in
all spell schools. Can advance dagger, axe, long sword, two handed
sword, hammer, and bare handed combat to expert. Can advance pick axe,
short sword, broad sword, polearm, spear, lance, riding, and two weapon
combat to skilled. For spells, limited to basic for attack & escape -
restricted in all other schools. Early game - suggest improving long
sword and dagger to skilled (4 slots) improving hit rates, adding about
20% damage on hits, and start giving you multiple dagger throws per
move. Dip for Excalibur if lawful; try to get skilled first - basic
rusty long swords and basic daggers can be very poor at killing a water
demon if summoned. Excalibur will greatly improve your long sword
damage & hit rates. At this point, if you have decent armor, it may be
helpful to remove the +3 shield and start training #twoweapon using
Excalibur and an enhanced dagger to skilled (3 slots). This leaves at
least 6 more skill slots available (at XL 14) to spread over the attack
& escape spell schools (2 slots), a distance weapon such as a polearm
or lance can be brought up to skilled (3 slots), and saber (1 slot -
for silver damage). If you have three extra skill slots, you could also
bring dagger to expert (3 slots) to improve multiple daggers thrown (up
to three) per move.

*Barbarian* Starts with basic in bare handed combat and the starting
axe & sword (varies) and unskilled in all spell schools. Can advance
bare handed combat to master. Can advance axe, short sword, two handed
sword, and hammer to expert. Can advance pick axe, broadsword, long
sword, scimitar, club, mace, morning star, spear, and trident to
skilled. For spells, can only advance attack spells to skilled -
restricted in all other schools. Early game - suggest using the primary
weapon (battle axe or two handed sword) and get to skilled (2 slots).
After that, what you do will often depend on what you get. If you get
an attack spell early (spellbook), train that up to skilled (2 slots).
After boosting your luck and finding (or making) a coaligned altar, its
OK to pray to get crowned and get Stormbringer and train broadsword to
skilled (3 slots). As an alternative to Stormbringer, get Cleaver (an
artifact gift from sacrifice) and use the battle axe skill; note that
Cleaver is two handed - try to prevent it getting cursed. Since those
suggestions use up to 7 slots (out of 13 at XL 14), advancing a few
other skills (e.g., dagger to basic - 1, spear to skilled - 6), gives
you some alternatives.

*Wizard* Starts with basic in Quarterstaff and basic in attack &
enchantment schools. Can advance dagger, quarterstaff, and dart to
expert. Can advance knife, axe, club, polearm, and sling to skilled.
For spells can advance healing, enchantment, & clerical schools to
skilled, can advance all the rest to expert. Early game - use your
spells & quarterstaff or daggers (collected from killed monsters) to
survive; suggest advancing the attack spells to skilled (1 slot) and
dagger to skilled (3 slots). I do not suggest advancing quarterstaff -
the slots are better used on spells and the two handed quarterstaff is
not likely your end game melee weapon. An artifact like Magicbane at
expert is a better choice. Advance the other spell schools as you learn
spells to basic (5 slots) to greatly improve spell failure rates. By XL
17, I suggest you bring dagger to expert (3 slots) for multiple daggers
per thrown and advance selected spell (e.g., diviniation, clerical,
escape) classes to skilled to get the enhanced effects and reduce
failure rates. If you choose to go to higher experience levels with a
wizard, advance attack & other spell schools you use to reduce failure
rates.

For other classes, see
(http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/343/spl1-343.html) for starting and
maximums for spell schools.



